## Radical innovation (advisory; not applied to the plan)

Put all the interference where the fit test can calibrate it.
- Make the bites a function of s. The full RIB_BITE_W and RIB_BITE_T apply only in a grip zone: the last GRIP_ZONE mm before B. The steel there is nearly straight (its radius of curvature is over 340 mm).
- Ramp the bites down to zero over RAMP mm. Over the curve, keep locating ribs with zero bite. They still set the glue gap and stop the tip from rattling.
- Set FIT_LEN to GRIP_ZONE + RAMP.

Results:
- The fit test has exactly the same contact as the whole grip, so how it feels predicts the full grip's seating and holding force one to one.
- Over the curve, rib squeeze during sliding drops to about zero, so binding there is designed out. That answers the plan's own pre-mortem ("the fit test fits but the full grip binds along the curve"), and A1 and A2 above.

Cost:
- ribs(h) takes a bite for each section; it already lofts section by section.
- B2 checks the bite in each zone.
- A sweep check asserts there is no interference outside the zone.

Risk: less area holds the grip. If a grip zone of about 15 to 20 mm does not hold, raise GRIP_ZONE.
